          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Lorck\",\"addressee\":\"Constance\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"This is an explicit, direct avowal that Lorck loves Constance. His immediate insistence that it is serious and more profound than he himself had realized reinforces its truth-presenting force, irrespective of sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The avowal begins an integrated romantic self-offer rather than merely standing next to an unrelated promise. Lorck presents his love as the basis for giving Constance himself, his life, and his soul, and asks her to accept that offered bond. This substantially supports undertaking force, although its practical terms are metaphorical and unspecified.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?*\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Although Lorck is emotionally agitated, this occurrence is a sustained and strategically formulated avowal and appeal after Constance resists him, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Emotional intensity alone does not establish E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Lorck korsede Armene paa Brystet, og\\nbetragtede hende stift.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"No substantial residual force is needed beyond avowal and relational undertaking. The appeal for acceptance is part of the romantic self-offer already represented under P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"vil du ikke ta imod det?*\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Lorck has confessed a consuming passion and Constance has resisted, recoiled, and asked him to stop, he directly repeats his declaration of love, stresses its seriousness, and offers her himself, his life, and his soul. The encounter is pressured and confrontational rather than mutually affectionate.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Lorck offers a comprehensive but metaphorically expressed romantic commitment—himself, his life, and his soul—and asks Constance to accept it. The declaration of love frames and grounds that offer within the same turn, so the undertaking is not a genuinely separate speech act. Constance explicitly rejects it with “Nej, uf nej” and moves back; no mutual bond is formed. The exact practical or sexual terms remain unspecified.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages identify the work as Amalie Skram’s Constance Ring, which I recognise.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly represents Lorck as emotionally affected, but it cannot independently prove the complete sincerity, durability, or precise intended terms of his declaration. Later supplied events should not be treated as knowledge available to either character at this moment.\"}"
